# death_if_fails rescaled for real per-act risk (drafts/repeats/rescale_death.py)
# Chroma library, modern Earth: THRESHOLD SEASONS, part 1 (Emren's point 6, card "Threshold season"): the crossings
# into juvenile (ages 11 to 13), young adult (17 to 20) and adult (24 to 32). Six moments per crossing: two at step 1
# (the crossing itself), two at step 2 with transform: yes (the in-between weeks, each with a chance that can change
# the character), two at step 3 (settling into the new life). Every moment is everyday, color-even by itself (the five
# one-color options plus one full cross cycle; build.py takes only cross cycles in everyday moments), and happens once.
# Brief: drafts/next2/BRIEF.md. Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: library-spec.md.
# Calls over the file: W 8, U 7, B 7, R 7, G 7 (all ten pairs at least once). Cycles: s1 5, s2 5, s3 4, s4 4.

# ================================================================ into juvenile (ages 11 to 13): leaving childhood

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 1, calls WG (cycle s1)

== the last afternoon of primary school
tier: everyday
at: school
stages: juvenile
age: 11-13
calls: WG
threshold: juvenile
step: 1
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: school, friends
horizon: moment
roles: friend, parent, a teacher, the little ones
earth: the last afternoon at primary school: shirts signed in felt pen, the teacher's goodbye, a gate you will not walk through again
times: Nearly every child, once, on the last afternoon of primary school at 10 to 12; most school systems move children to a bigger school around then (estimate)
scene: On the last afternoon of primary school the class signs each other's shirts in felt pen, and the teacher who has known {N} since the first day keeps finding reasons to look out of the window.
scene W: There is a card for the teacher with every name in the class on it, and someone has to stand up and give it. {N} has practised the words all week.
scene U: {N} looks round the classroom and tries to fix it in memory: the crack in the window, the reading corner, the smell of the radiator. In September none of it will be theirs.
scene B: Next year {N} will be the smallest again. For one more afternoon {N} is among the oldest in the building, and the little ones still step aside in the corridor.
scene R: The bell goes at three, and then it is summer, and then everything changes. {N} wants to scream and cry at the same time.
scene G: {N} walks past the coat peg that has carried {Ns} name since the first week. {friend} is beside {N}, the way {friend} always has been.
- W | stand up and give the teacher the class card, with the words you practised | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 85
- U | write a letter to yourself, sealed, to be opened when you turn eighteen | v: self-direction | act: write a letter to themselves, sealed, to be opened when they turn eighteen | chance: 95
- B | get the teacher to write a glowing note about you for the new school | v: achievement | act: get the teacher to write a glowing note about them for the new school | chance: 80
- R | spray the others with water at the gate and run off shrieking into the summer | body: light | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 88
- G | walk round the playground one last time and say goodbye to each corner of it | v: tradition | chance: 95
- W>U | ask the teacher what the big school will really be like, and listen to every word | v: self-direction, security | chance: 88
- U>B | find out who else is going to your new school, and decide who to stick with | v: security, power | chance: 80
- B>R | sell your old textbooks to the year below at the gate, and spend it on summer | v: hedonism, achievement | chance: 70
- R>G | hug your best friend, crying, and swear that nothing between the two of you will change | binds | v: benevolence | act: hug their best friend, crying, and swear that nothing between the two of them will change | role: women | chance: 72
- G>W | stay behind to help take down the old displays, as the leavers always have | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 88
works: The afternoon ends with everyone at the gate, and {N} walks home in a shirt covered in names.
works: {N} keeps the day whole, and years later can still call up every part of it.
fails: The bell goes before {N} has said goodbye to anyone who mattered.
fails: Something small goes wrong in front of the whole school, and it is the thing {N} remembers.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 1, calls UB (cycle s2)

== the first morning at the big school
tier: everyday
at: school
stages: juvenile
age: 11-13
calls: UB
threshold: juvenile
step: 1
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: school
horizon: moment
roles: friend, parent, a form tutor, the older pupils
earth: the first morning at secondary school: a timetable you cannot read, four floors of corridors, and pupils who look like grown-ups
times: Nearly every child, once, on the first morning at secondary or middle school, at 10 to 12; in England nearly all pupils start secondary school at 11 (estimate)
scene: On the first morning at the big school {N} stands in a corridor with a timetable that makes no sense, while pupils who look like grown adults stream past on both sides.
scene W: The uniform is stiff, the tie took three tries, and the school rules are printed inside the planner. {N} has read them twice on the bus.
scene U: Four floors, three staircases and a room called L14 that seems not to exist. {N} unfolds the map and starts working out the logic of the building.
scene B: Within an hour {N} can see who runs the corridors, which table in the canteen belongs to whom, and which older pupils even the teachers handle carefully.
scene R: Nobody here holds {Ns} hand to cross anything. The bus, the gates, a thousand new faces: {N} feels the size of it like a plunge into cold water.
scene G: {friend} from the old class is in the same form, and {N} keeps {friend} in sight all morning like a landmark on a hill.
- W | arrive early, find the form room, and sit exactly where the tutor points | v: conformity, security | chance: 92
- U | spend the first break walking the whole building until you know every staircase | v: self-direction | chance: 88
- B | make yourself useful to the older pupil who seems to run the corridor | v: power, security | act: make themselves useful to the older pupil who seems to run the corridor | chance: 70
- R | get lost on purpose and look behind the doors marked staff only | v: stimulation | closed: approval: going where pupils are not allowed; backfire: caught, and a detention in the first week | chance: 65
- G | stay close to the one friend from your old class all day | v: security | chance: 75
- W>B | put your hand up to be form representative on the very first day | v: power, achievement | chance: 45
- U>R | read the list of lunchtime clubs and sign up for the strangest one on it | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 85
- B>G | gather the kids from your street into a crew that walks home together | v: security | mark: made a friend | chance: 85
- R>W | step in when a small kid is being shoved on the stairs | body: light | v: benevolence, universalism | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 65
- G>U | ask the caretaker, who has been here thirty years, how the place really works | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 75
works: By lunchtime {N} knows where the toilets are, has a seat at a table, and has not cried once.
works: The day goes better than {N} feared, and on the bus home it is all {N} can talk about.
fails: {N} is late to three lessons and gets the same telling-off three times.
fails: Someone older picks {N} out on the first morning, and the day becomes something to get through.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 2, transform, calls BR (cycle s3)

== nobody at the new school knows who you were
tier: everyday
at: school
stages: juvenile
age: 11-13
calls: BR
threshold: juvenile
step: 2
transform: yes
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: school, self
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, sibling, the new classmates
earth: a few weeks into the new school you realise that nobody here knows the nickname, the bad year or the quiet kid you used to be
times: Many children, once, a few weeks into secondary school; most of all those who arrive knowing few of their new classmates (estimate)
scene: A few weeks in, it dawns on {N} that nobody here knows about the nickname, the crying at sports day, or the year {N} hardly spoke. {N} could be anyone.
scene W: At the old school {N} was always the one who got told off. Here the record is clean, and {N} wonders what it would be like to be the one the teachers trust.
scene U: {N} watches how the others decide who is who in the first weeks, from the clothes, the jokes and the speed of an answer, and sees that all of it can be learned.
scene B: Nobody here knows that {Ns} family has no car, or which estate {N} comes from. The story of who {N} is has not been told yet, so {N} can tell it.
scene R: The old self feels like a coat that never fitted. {N} wants to throw it off all at once and see who is underneath.
scene G: {N} catches a reflection in the bus window: taller, the face changing. Whatever {N} is becoming is already on its way, whatever anyone decides.
- W | join the scouts or guides and make the promise, uniform and all | identity | binds | v: conformity, tradition | title: scout or guide | chance: 85
- U | choose the person you mean to become, and practise being them every day | identity | habit | v: self-direction | self_control: + | act: choose the person they mean to become, and practise being that person every day | chance: 55
- B | invent a better past: a famous uncle, a house abroad, a fight you won | identity | v: power | chance: 62
- R | go by your middle name, cut your hair short, and answer to nothing else | identity | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 75
- G | stay exactly who you were, and let the right people find you | identity | v: tradition, security | mark: turned down a chance | act: stay exactly who they were, and let the right people find them | chance: 60
- W>R | audition for the school play, the last thing anyone who knew you would expect | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | act: audition for the school play, the last thing anyone who knew them would expect | chance: 50
- U>G | spend the evenings finding out who you are, in a notebook nobody reads | habit | v: self-direction | chance: 85
- B>W | make friends with the teachers' pet, and become one of the trusted ones | v: power, conformity | chance: 70
- R>U | throw yourself into the one subject that lights you up, and be known for it | v: achievement, self-direction | grants: teachers' favourite | act: throw themselves into the one subject that lights them up, and be known for it | self_control: + | chance: 75
- G>B | lean on your older cousins' name, so nobody at the new school dares touch you | v: power, security | act: lean on their older cousins' name, so nobody at the new school dares touch them | chance: 55
works: By half term the new {N} feels less like a costume and more like a fit.
works: The others take {N} the way {N} chooses to be seen, and something in {N} settles.
fails: Someone from the old school turns up in the same class, and the old story arrives with them.
fails: The new self is hard to keep up, and by the end of term {N} is worn out by it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 2, transform, calls RG (cycle s4)

== a friend whose family lives on the river
tier: everyday
stages: juvenile
age: 11-13
calls: RG
threshold: juvenile
step: 2
transform: yes
once: yes
tone: joy
life: friends, nature, home
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, the friend's family
earth: a new friend from the big school lives on a narrowboat with a family who keep no clock, and wants you aboard every weekend
times: Rare: perhaps 1 child in 20 or fewer, once, in the first months at the new school; few families live aboard a boat or as freely as this (estimate)
scene: {friend}, a new friend from the big school, lives on a narrowboat with a family who cook on a wood stove, keep no clock and swim off the back of the boat. Every Friday {friend} asks {N} to come aboard for the weekend.
scene W: {Ns} home runs on mealtimes, homework and bedtimes. On the boat nobody sets a bedtime, and {N} cannot decide whether that is wonderful or wrong.
scene U: The boat runs on a solar panel, a water tank and a dozen tricks that {friend}'s mother explains as she goes. {N} wants to know how every one of them works.
scene B: {friend}'s family owns almost nothing and seems to need nothing. {N} watches them swap eggs for diesel with the next boat along, and takes note.
scene R: Leaping off the roof into the canal, fires on the towpath, whole days outdoors: {N} has never felt so free.
scene G: A heron at dawn, a kingfisher under the bridge, the water moving under the bunk all night. {N} sleeps on the boat better than anywhere.
- W | promise to keep up at school in return for weekends aboard, and keep it | v: conformity, security | mark: kept your word | self_control: + | chance: 75
- U | learn everything the boat can teach: knots, engines, locks and weather | v: self-direction | grants: boat handling | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
- B | make the boat a second home, and play the two homes off against each other | v: self-direction, power | chance: 72
- R | go every weekend and the whole half-term, whatever anyone says at home | habit | v: stimulation, hedonism | mark: defied an authority | closed: approval: against the parents' wishes; backfire: grounded, and a row that lasts | chance: 70
- G | let the river slow you down, and learn to sit still and watch the water | habit | v: universalism (nature) | act: let the river slow them down, and learn to sit still and watch the water | self_control: + | chance: 60
- W>G | bring your family down to the towpath to meet them, so both homes are one | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 55
- U>W | promise to help your friend every week with the schoolwork the boat life misses | binds | v: benevolence, achievement | chance: 65
- B>U | trade your old games to the mechanic on the next boat for lessons on the engine | door | v: achievement, self-direction | grants: fixing things | mark: made a friend | chance: 75
- R>B | take the tiller without asking, to show off to the older kids on the towpath | body: light | v: power, stimulation | closed: approval: steering without leave; backfire: a scrape on the lock gate, and the family's trust gone | chance: 68
- G>R | follow the river with your friend for a whole day, wherever it goes | body: light | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | closed: approval: gone all day without telling anyone; backfire: a search, and a ban on the boat | chance: 55
works: The boat becomes a second world for {N}, and {N} comes home from it a little different each time.
works: Both families come round to it, and {N} has the river as well as home.
fails: The boat moves on to another stretch of water, and the weekends go with it.
fails: A row at home puts the boat out of bounds, and {friend} drifts away with it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 3, calls UR (cycle s1)

== the first Saturday in town without a grown-up
tier: everyday
stages: juvenile
age: 11-13
calls: UR
threshold: juvenile
step: 3
once: yes
tone: joy
life: friends, freedom, money
horizon: moment
roles: friend, parent, grandparent
earth: the first Saturday you may go into town with friends on the bus, with a little money, a phone and a time to be home by
times: Most children, once, at about 11 to 13, the first time they may go into town with friends alone; that age has risen since the 1970s (Policy Studies Institute, children's independent mobility)
scene: For the first time {N} is allowed into town on the bus with {friend}, with a little money, a charged phone and a promise to be home by five.
scene W: {parent} made {N} repeat the rules at the door: stay together, keep the phone on, home by five. {N} means to keep every one of them.
scene U: {N} has the bus times saved and a plan for the day: the comic shop, the market, the top floor of the library, in that order.
scene B: Money in a pocket and nobody to say no: {N} has wanted the trainers in the sports-shop window for months, and today is the day to work out how.
scene R: The bus doors fold open on the high street, and the whole town is noise and possibility. Nobody in the world knows exactly where {N} is.
scene G: {N} walks the streets {grandparent} walked at the same age, past the same bakery and under the same clock on the town hall.
- W | keep every rule you promised, and text home at each stop | v: conformity, security | mark: kept your word | tech: phone | chance: 85
- U | find your way round town without looking at the map, to test yourself | v: self-direction | act: find their way round town without looking at the map, as a test | chance: 75
- B | spend it all on the one thing that will impress the others on Monday | v: power, hedonism | self_control: - | chance: 90
- R | change the plan halfway and jump on a bus to wherever it goes | v: stimulation | closed: approval: off the agreed route; backfire: lost, with a dead phone | chance: 72
- G | spend the afternoon on the grass by the river, just sitting with your friends | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 92
- W>U | get a library card in your own name, signed and stamped | door | v: self-direction, conformity | chance: 92
- U>B | work out which usher never checks, and get into the older film | v: hedonism, self-direction | closed: approval: an age-rated film; backfire: turned out, and home told | chance: 70
- B>R | talk the market trader down to half price, and spend the rest on chips | v: hedonism, achievement | chance: 70
- R>G | buy a goldfish on impulse and carry it home on the bus in its bag | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 88
- G>W | buy a small present for home with the change, as your grandparent always did | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 88
works: {N} comes home on time, with a bag, a story and the feeling of being trusted.
works: The day goes so well that next Saturday is agreed before tea.
fails: The money and the time run out together, and {parent} has to come into town to fetch {N}.
fails: Something goes wrong in town, and it is a while before {N} is let go alone again.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 3, calls WB (cycle s2)

== the table where you sit now
tier: everyday
at: school
stages: juvenile
age: 11-13
calls: WB
threshold: juvenile
step: 3
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: friends, school
horizon: months
roles: friend, grandparent, rival, the new crowd
earth: by the second term you have a table in the canteen and a crowd of your own, and your best friend from the old school sits at another one
times: Most children, once, in the first terms at secondary school, as friendships regroup; many primary school friendships fade after the move (estimate)
scene: By the second term {N} has a regular table in the canteen and a crowd to sit with. {friend}, the best friend from the old school, sits two tables away with other people now.
scene W: Every table has its rules about who sits where, who saves whose seat and who gets asked. {N} knows them now, and knows {friend} is no longer inside them.
scene U: {N} watches the canteen like a map: who moved table this week, who is drifting, who brought {friend} into their group and why.
scene B: {Ns} new table is one along from the window, and the window table is where the people everyone watches sit. One more step and {N} is there.
scene R: Sometimes {N} looks across at {friend} laughing with strangers and feels it like a stitch in the side.
scene G: Friends come and go like the seasons, {grandparent} says. {N} is not sure yet whether to believe it.
- W | ask the new kid who eats alone to come and sit at your table | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 75
- U | keep a friend at every table, and belong to none of them completely | v: self-direction | chance: 60
- B | drop the friends who are going nowhere, and move up to the window table | v: power, achievement | mark: made an enemy | chance: 60
- R | march over to your old friend's table and sit down as if nothing has changed | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 70
- G | let the old friendship rest, trusting it is still there underneath | v: security, tradition | chance: 55
- W>B | tell the head about the window table's vaping, and move in when they are gone | v: power, conformity | closed: approval: telling on classmates; backfire: everyone learns who told | chance: 55
- U>R | start a lunchtime club for the game you love, and see who turns up | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 55
- B>G | keep your old friend close in secret, while being seen with the new crowd | v: security, benevolence | chance: 70
- R>W | tell your table to stop mocking a kid from your old school, and mean it | v: universalism, benevolence | chance: 70
- G>U | ask your grandparent how they kept their oldest friend for sixty years | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 80
works: {N} ends the year with a place that feels like {Ns} own, and nobody who mattered left behind.
works: The tables settle, and {N} finds that a friendship can change shape without ending.
fails: {N} picks a side, and the other side remembers.
fails: By summer {N} has a table but nobody at it {N} could call in trouble.

# ================================================================ into young adult (ages 17 to 20): leaving school and home

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 1, calls WR (cycle s3)

== pens down on the last exam
tier: everyday
at: school
stages: young_adult
age: 17-20
calls: WR
threshold: young adult
step: 1
once: yes
tone: joy
life: school, friends
horizon: moment
roles: friend, parent, a teacher, an uncle
earth: the invigilator says pens down on your very last school exam, and outside the hall it is summer
times: Most young people, once, at 16 to 19, at the end of their last school exams; about 9 in 10 US adults have finished high school (Census)
scene: The invigilator says pens down on {Ns} very last exam, and thirteen years of school stop in a single breath. Outside the hall it is June.
scene W: {N} lays the pen beside the paper, waits to be dismissed and files out in line, as {N} has done a hundred times. This is the last time.
scene U: Question seven is still turning over in {Ns} head. Somewhere between the hall and the car park {N} realises that it no longer matters to anyone.
scene B: Everything {N} has done for years has been aimed at the grades. Now the grades are out of {Ns} hands, and the next move is {Ns} own.
scene R: The doors crash open and the whole year pours out into the sun, ties off, bottles fizzing, somebody already in the fountain.
scene G: {N} looks back at the school from the gate: the windows, the playing field, the hedge with the gap in it. It is already the past.
- W | go back in and thank the teachers who got you through, one by one | v: benevolence, tradition | act: go back in and thank the teachers who got them through, one by one | chance: 90
- U | check every answer online that same night, question by question | v: achievement, security | self_control: - | tech: internet | chance: 95
- B | go straight to the job you lined up, and start earning that same afternoon | v: achievement, power | self_control: + | chance: 92
- R | cram into a friend's car and race the others to the coast, music up | v: hedonism, stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | death_if_fails: 0.003 | chance: 85
- G | sit on the field where you sat at eleven, and let it all sink in | v: tradition | chance: 88
- W>R | organise the whole year's week of camping on the coast, as everyone promised | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 68
- U>G | set off on the long coast walk you mapped out during revision | v: universalism (nature), self-direction | chance: 85
- B>W | ask the head for a reference now, while your name is still fresh | v: achievement, security | chance: 85
- R>U | on a whim, sign up for the summer language course that starts on Monday | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 82
- G>B | ring your uncle tonight and take the job in the family trade he always promised | v: security, achievement | chance: 70
works: {N} walks out into the light feeling lighter than in years.
works: The day turns into a night {N} will tell stories about for the rest of a lifetime.
fails: The relief does not come; {N} feels oddly empty, as if something has been taken away.
fails: The night gets out of hand, and the morning brings a mess to sort out.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 1, calls BG (cycle s4)

== the morning you leave home
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult
age: 17-20
calls: BG
threshold: young adult
step: 1
once: yes
moves: yes
tone: mixed
life: home, family
horizon: moment
roles: parent, sibling, grandparent
earth: the car is packed to the roof, your room is stripped to the mattress, and today you leave home to live on your own
times: Nearly everyone, once, the first time they move out of the parental home, often at 18 to 25; the EU average age is about 26, nearer 21 in the Nordic countries (Eurostat)
scene: The car is packed to the roof, {Ns} room is stripped to the bare mattress, and {parent} has been up since five, pretending to tidy.
scene W: {N} has a folder: the tenancy, the bank card, a list of what goes where. Leaving home is a job to be done properly.
scene U: {N} looks round the bare room and tries to work out which things were truly {Ns} and which only came with the house.
scene B: No more asking, no more curfews, no more queueing for the bathroom behind {sibling}: from tonight {N} answers to nobody.
scene R: {N} has wanted this day for years, and now that it is here {Ns} throat is tight and {N} cannot look at {parent}.
scene G: {grandparent} has come round to wave {N} off, the way the family has always seen its young ones go, and presses something folded into {Ns} hand.
- W | say a proper goodbye, and promise to ring home every Sunday | habit | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 80
- U | pack only what you used this year, and leave the rest behind for good | v: self-direction | chance: 90
- B | take the good duvet, the toaster and anything else nobody will miss | v: hedonism, power | chance: 90
- R | go quickly, without a long goodbye, while you still can | v: self-direction | act: go quickly, without a long goodbye, while they still can | chance: 90
- G | take a cutting from the garden and a jar of the family's jam | v: tradition | chance: 92
- W>G | let your parents drive you there and make up the new bed, as they want to | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 88
- U>W | brief your younger sibling on everything they need to know about your parents | v: benevolence | chance: 88
- B>U | sell your old things online first, to pay for a term of books | v: achievement | tech: internet | chance: 85
- R>B | tell your parents you will never need their money again | identity | v: self-direction, power | chance: 50
- G>R | set off with nothing but a rucksack, the way your grandparent left their village | v: self-direction, tradition | chance: 70
works: {N} unpacks the first box in the new room, and the strangeness turns slowly into a beginning.
works: The goodbye is the right size, and {N} leaves with something settled between {N} and home.
fails: The goodbye goes badly, and the first night in the new room is lonely in a way {N} did not expect.
fails: Something that mattered is left behind, and the first week starts with a panic.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 2, transform, calls RG (cycle s1)

== work and a room abroad, if you go this month
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult
age: 17-20
calls: RG
threshold: young adult
step: 2
transform: yes
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: work, travel, family
horizon: years
roles: parent, friend, a cousin abroad
earth: a cousin abroad has a job in the family restaurant and a room above it for you, if you can be on a plane before the month is out
times: Rare: perhaps 1 in 20 young people, once, at 17 to 20, gets an offer like this; about 1 in 28 people alive lives outside their country of birth (UN DESA 2020)
scene: A cousin abroad has a job going in the family's restaurant and a spare room above it, if {N} can be on a plane before the month is out.
scene W: {N} has a place at college in September and a promise to {parent} to take it. Going would break a plan everyone agreed on.
scene U: A new language, a new country, a whole other way of living to learn from the inside. {N} has already found three phrasebooks and a map of the town.
scene B: The pay is better than anything at home, the rent is almost nothing, and the cousin knows people. A year there could set {N} up.
scene R: Sun, the sea, a life nobody at home can picture. {N} said yes in {Ns} heart before the message had finished.
scene G: The cousin's family is {Ns} own family, only further away: the same recipes, the same arguments, a branch of the tree {N} has never met.
- W | turn it down and take up the college place, as agreed | v: conformity, security | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 85
- U | go for a year, learn the language properly, and come back with it | door | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: moved away | grants: second language | chance: 70
- B | go, work every hour there is, and send nothing home until you have a stake | habit | v: achievement, power | mark: moved away | grants: savings | self_control: + | chance: 70
- R | book the flight tonight and tell everyone afterwards | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: moved away | title: waiter or bartender | tech: plane | chance: 75
- G | go, and live with that branch of the family while it feels like home | door | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: moved away | grants: family abroad | chance: 68
- W>U | defer the college place for a year, properly and in writing, and then go | v: self-direction, security | mark: moved away | chance: 65
- U>B | go, and quietly learn the cousin's recipes and suppliers for a place of your own | v: achievement, power | mark: moved away | closed: approval: taking the family's trade secrets; backfire: a feud that splits the family | chance: 65
- B>R | go without a work permit, and take your chances with cash jobs | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: broke the law | closed: law: working without a permit; backfire: found out, sent home and barred from returning | chance: 65
- R>G | go, fall for the place, and decide there and then to put down roots | v: security, self-direction | title: immigrant | chance: 45
