earth: the builder you laboured for over the summer offers you an apprenticeship that starts on Monday, the same week as the course you signed up for
times: Some school leavers, once, at 16 to 20; about half of young Germans start an apprenticeship (BIBB), far fewer in Britain or the US
scene: {mentor}, the builder {N} laboured for over the summer, rings on a Thursday: there is an apprenticeship going, it starts on Monday, and it is {Ns} if {N} wants it. The college course starts the same week.
scene W: An apprenticeship is a contract: four years, a trade and a ticket at the end. {N} likes the sound of something with a shape that clear.
scene U: {N} has always learned faster with something in hand than with a book. The course is the theory; the apprenticeship is the real thing.
scene B: An apprentice earns from the first week, while friends at college run up debts. {N} does the sums on the back of a receipt.
scene R: {N} cannot stand the thought of three more years behind a desk. On the site last summer every single day felt like doing something.
scene G: {Ns} grandfather was a joiner, and his tools are still in a box in {Ns} grandmother's shed. {N} has been thinking about that box all week.
