works: {N} discovers a small, solid pride in paying {Ns} own way.
fails: The money runs out a week before the month does.
fails: An unexpected bill eats the lot, and {N} has to ring home and ask.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 3, calls UG (cycle s4)

== the first weekend back, and the town looks smaller
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult
age: 17-20
calls: UG
threshold: young adult
step: 3
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: home, family, friends
horizon: week
roles: parent, grandparent, friend, an old teacher
earth: the first weekend you go back to your home town after leaving, and everything is the same except you
times: Most people who leave home, once, on the first visit back in the months after moving out; nearly all go back to visit (estimate)
scene: On {Ns} first weekend back, the town is exactly as it was: the same bus, the same bakery, {parent} at the same window. Only {N} has changed, and everyone can tell.
scene W: {N} is home for Sunday lunch, as promised, and finds a place laid at the table as if {N} had never left.
scene U: {N} sees things never noticed while growing up: how short the high street is, how {parent} has aged, how every conversation runs along the same few tracks.
scene B: The friends who stayed are in the same pub, with the same jobs and the same plans. {N} notices a quiet pleasure in having got out.
scene R: {Ns} old bedroom has become the room for the ironing. {N} sits on the bed among the shirts and does not know whether to laugh or cry.
scene G: The smell of the hall, the creak of the third stair, the dog going mad at the door: home takes {N} back without asking a single question.
- W | give in, and promise to come home every weekend, as your parents ask | v: conformity | mark: gave in to pressure | self_control: - | chance: 75
- U | notice how home has changed, and work out how much of the change is in you | v: self-direction | act: notice how home has changed, and work out how much of the change is in them | chance: 85
- B | show the old crowd how well you are doing, and pay for every round | v: power | chance: 90
- R | stay out all Saturday night with the old crowd, as if you never left | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 88
- G | walk the fields behind the house, and let home settle back into you | v: tradition, universalism (nature) | act: walk the fields behind the house, and let home settle back into them | chance: 90
- W>G | drive up through the night after your shift, so as not to miss Sunday lunch | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: kept your word | death_if_fails: 0.002 | tech: car | chance: 75
- U>W | notice that your grandparent is struggling, and quietly arrange help from the council | v: benevolence, security | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 70
- B>U | look up an old teacher with contacts in the city, and ask for introductions | door | v: achievement | chance: 70
- R>B | tell the friends who stayed exactly what you think of this town, and leave early | v: self-direction, power | mark: made an enemy | chance: 75
- G>R | swim across the lake at night with the old crowd, as every summer | body: heavy | v: stimulation, tradition | mark: took a wild risk | death_if_fails: 0.002 | closed: law: swimming where it is banned; backfire: fined, or pulled out of the water | chance: 75
works: The weekend reminds {N} where {N} comes from, and {N} goes back to the new life steadier for it.
works: Home and {N} find a new way of fitting together, a little looser than before.
fails: The weekend ends in a row, and {N} catches the train back early with a hot face.
fails: {N} feels a stranger in both places, and the journey back is long.

# ================================================================ into adult (ages 24 to 32): leaving youth

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 1, calls BR (cycle s1)

== the birthday party that ends at eleven
tier: everyday
stages: adult
age: 24-32
calls: BR
threshold: adult
step: 1
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: friends, self
horizon: moment
roles: friend, parent, partner, sibling
earth: at your birthday party half the guests leave at eleven, for babysitters and early starts, and it feels as if youth has quietly ended
times: Many adults, once, in their late twenties or early thirties, as friends start families; mothers average nearly 30 at a first birth in the EU (Eurostat) and about 27 in the US (CDC)
scene: At {Ns} birthday party half the guests leave at eleven, for babysitters, early starts and long drives home. {N} stands in the kitchen with a glass and the sudden sense that something has ended.
scene W: {friend} gives a toast about the years they have shared, and {N} hears in it the outline of a settled life: the job, the flat, the people who stay.
scene U: {N} counts it up, half amused: a pension statement, a dentist twice a year, opinions about gutters. When did all of that happen?
scene B: Around the room {N} sees who has got on and who has not: a promotion here, a house there. {N} quietly works out where {N} stands among them.
scene R: It is not even midnight. {N} refuses to let the night, or the decade, end like this.
scene G: {parent} sends a photo of {N} at one year old, cake all over a high chair. {N} looks at it for a long time.
- W | thank everyone who came with a toast, and see the last ones safely out | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 92
- U | take stock on the walk home: what you wanted at twenty, and what you have | v: self-direction | chance: 90
- B | take whatever is going round with the last few guests, and keep the night alive | v: hedonism, stimulation | mark: used drugs | closed: law: illegal drugs; backfire: a bad night, a lost week, or worse | death_if_fails: 0.002 | law: drugs | chance: 82
- R | drag the last guests out to a club and dance until it closes | body: light | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 80
- G | sit out on the step at the end, alone, and let the decade go | v: tradition (acceptance) | chance: 90
- W>U | book the health check you keep putting off, as a birthday present to yourself | v: security | self_control: + | act: book the health check they keep putting off, as a birthday present to themselves | chance: 90
- U>B | ask the friend who got on fastest exactly how they did it | v: achievement | chance: 85
- B>R | decide to stop spending your years on people who bore you, from tonight | v: self-direction, hedonism | act: decide to stop spending their years on people who bore them, from tonight | chance: 80
- R>G | dance in the kitchen with the friends who stayed, to the same songs as at nineteen | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 90
- G>W | ring your parents, late as it is, and thank them for all of it | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 80
works: The night ends well, and {N} wakes oddly at peace with the years behind.
works: Something {N} said or did that night turns out to be the start of the next decade.
fails: The night ends badly, and the next day goes on apologies.
fails: {N} wakes with the feeling of having missed something, and it stays for weeks.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- step 1, calls WU (cycle s2)

== a contract with no end date
tier: everyday
at: employer
stages: adult
age: 24-32
calls: WU
threshold: adult
step: 1
once: yes
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: years
roles: boss, colleague, parent, partner
earth: after years of temping and short contracts you are offered a permanent post with a pension, and it needs signing by Friday
times: Many adults, once, in their twenties or early thirties; about 1 in 7 EU employees, and nearly half of those under 25, are on temporary contracts (Eurostat)
scene: After years of temping, short contracts and rent paid late, {N} has a permanent contract on the desk: a pension, sick pay, and no end date. {boss} wants it signed by Friday.
scene W: A permanent post means being counted on, and counting on something in return. {N} reads the pension clause three times, the way one reads a vow.
scene U: {N} reads every clause, then the staff handbook, then the rules of the pension scheme, and ends up with a list of questions longer than the contract.
scene B: They want {N} enough to offer a permanent post. That means {N} has something to bargain with, and Friday is a deadline for both sides.
scene R: No end date. {N} feels the words close round like a door, and also, oddly, like a floor to stand on.
scene G: {parent} worked for the same firm for thirty-one years and got a clock at the end. {N} looks at the signature line and thinks of that clock.
